{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261126T060528Z-6d650c59", "ts": "2026-11-26T06:08:41Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.10.0", "branch": "fix/critic-good-faith-severity", "build": "e9c06c071287"}, "body": {"base_commit": "05a1dceb997d0a861f951c64bc721e9939b3d6e8", "base_tree": "e0ff9a692c92b44674b96f9c8bc8f6045a4f4e05", "head_tree": "205dfea816304e1035489c0ceca880f86ed312bc", "head_commit": "b4e4df412762bbe677540a148e4de99507f00eef", "dispatch_commit": "b4e4df412762bbe677540a148e4de99507f00eef", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "rule-2 cumulative: branch is 2 commits ahead of origin/develop, working tree clean, no fresh cumulative-mode record for current HEAD, and no composed review coverage spans the branch", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "tests/test_good_faith_severity.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/changes/critic-good-faith-severity.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "tests/test_good_faith_severity.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/changes/critic-good-faith-severity.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "The good-faith cap collides with 'the code violates this spec stays at full severity' when a product's requirements name a bad-faith actor", "recommendation": "Framework Checks 7 (Generality) and 8 (Instruction Clarity). The owner's design stance (.prawduct/owner-rulings.md, 2026-11-26) is about the framework's own defenses: gates against a builder hiding work, grants against a model invoking a skill unasked. The shipped bullet has no such scope. It reaches every product repo's review and caps 'a finding whose failure needs a person acting in bad faith ... whatever its goal says'. The same files also say \"The code violates this spec\" has the code as its subject, at full severity (review-protocol.md Scope and Stage; goals-1-3.md Before you review step 1). On a product whose requirements defend against a bad-faith actor, the two rules give opposite ratings and neither says which wins. Examples of such a product: insider-tamper-evident audit logs, fraud or abuse controls on authenticated users, anti-cheat. The floor's 'untrusted input' covers an attacker across a trust boundary, and the change-log says that is the intent ('an attacker across a trust boundary is a real failure mode, not the bad faith this rule is about'). But that clarification is only in the oracle record. An insider with legitimate access is not obviously 'untrusted input'. The consequence: a reviewer on such a product caps an unmet threat-model requirement to an observation, it gates nothing, and it ships. This matches the authoring rule that begins 'A ruling recorded only where the deciders read it is NOT recorded'. Instance fix through the one pinned line: add the product's own threat model to the floor sentence, for example '...untrusted input, an actor the product's requirements defend against, or competent mistake keeps its severity'. Alternatively, scope 'a person' to whoever operates the governed workflow (builder, owner, reviewing model). Update FLOOR in tests/test_good_faith_severity.py to match. If the owner meant the cap to reach product threat models too, record that scope in owner-rulings.md instead and leave the bullet alone.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "tests/test_good_faith_severity.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "Each carrier of the pinned good-faith-cap bullet; there are exactly two, held identical by test_both_carriers_state_one_rule.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md \u00a7 Severity Levels, 'Good faith caps severity'"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md \u00a7 Severity, 'Good faith caps severity'"}], "fid": "R-1"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 1, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "The cap's 'a model ignoring its instructions' trigger can be read as covering the builder's own conduct in the diff", "recommendation": "Genuinely ambiguous, so this is an observation and no edit is owed. The cap is meant for a finding whose failure needs a future actor to act in bad faith. A diff where the builder model exceeded or ignored its plan (Goal 3's extra functionality WARNING, its undocumented-architectural-decision BLOCKING) literally fits 'a model ignoring its instructions', but there the defect is already in the tree. A careful reviewer reads 'whose failure needs' correctly, and 'competent mistake' in the floor covers most of these cases. If a capped Goal 3 rating ever turns up in a review, tighten the clause to the failure the reviewed code permits, not the conduct that produced it.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation: no governed_by disposition to reconcile; the normative change rests on recorded owner rulings", "recommendation": "No live plan claims the branch, so no governed_by disposition exists to test against the diff. This is a normative change to the Critic protocol, and it rests on recorded vetoable decisions: item 4 of .prawduct/owner-rulings.md '2026-11-26 (what was over-built)', and the '2026-11-26 (design stance)' ruling, which supplies the open-set clause ('trying to close an open set makes no sense') and the floor (crashes, git errors, stale state). 'untrusted input' and 'competent mistake' only preserve severities, which matches the ruling's 'accidents and real failure modes'. No edit wanted.", "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "In-place cuts checked against their claimed owners; nothing load-bearing removed", "recommendation": "Each cut sentence is still stated where its commit comment says. The inner-stage restatement is in review-protocol.md's Stage paragraph. The oracle-order ownership is in the Scope paragraph's pointer to cross-checks.md, and the Observation bullet's prose cap covers subject files only. 'Target 4-10 min' is in review-cycle.md's Target wall-clock row. The goals-1-3.md record-check and norm-departure bullets merged into the paragraphs above them. The round-cost clause is in the preamble's 'fixing one re-opens the gate and costs a round'. Grepping tests/ and plugin/ finds no test or consumer pinning the removed text. Recorded test evidence covers this exact tree (205dfea8): 13694 passed, 0 failed. The budget deltas add up (+56 and +78, and the six payload rows match the carrier each payload loads). The control test feeds the one-sentence predicate a split floor and confirms it catches it. No edit wanted.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "tests/test_good_faith_severity.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The cap's parenthetical examples are framework-internal and opaque to a product reviewer", "recommendation": "Check 7, slight specificity. 'Rewriting a version file into a command', 'invoking a skill unasked' and 'every spelling a prefix grant reaches' are prawduct's own retired defenses. A reviewer of a web app or firmware gets the principle but no example from its own world. The general property is stated, so this does not harm generality, and the examples are pinned by nothing but the identity test. No edit wanted unless the bullet is touched for the finding above.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "'whatever its goal says' does not literally cover the Framework-Specific Checks or the Records Pass bars", "recommendation": "framework-checks.md and cross-checks.md assign severities through 'severity guides' and 'bars', not goals. A literal reader could treat those as outside the cap. A competent reader applies the cap anyway, since it sits in the shared Severity Levels section, so this is wording only. If the bullet is edited for the finding above, 'whatever its goal or check says' would close it at no cost.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The in-place cuts that paid for the cap leave no dangling references", "recommendation": "Checked every cut against its claimed owner. Inner-stage BLOCKING scoping: the Stage paragraph. Oracle order: Scope's 'cross-checks.md is the one home'. `final`'s 4-10 min: review-cycle.md's Target wall-clock row. Norm-departure Goal 3 bullet: the Normative authority paragraph and the inner BLOCKING set; its remedy moved into the Tell. Record-checks Goal 2 bullet: the record_lint paragraph, which now carries 'chunk deliverables included'. A grep for the removed phrasings found no surface pointing at them. No edit wanted.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The new test reuses the mode-to-carrier map rather than restating it", "recommendation": "tests/test_good_faith_severity.py imports ALL_MODES and DOCUMENTED from test_finding_scope_rule. DOCUMENTED is parsed from review-cycle.md's Protocol read row, so a re-route is picked up without editing this test. Cross-test imports have precedent in this suite. The local _severity_section helper is one more of several near-identical section slicers across tests, a pre-existing pattern too small to extract here. No edit wanted.", "files": ["tests/test_good_faith_severity.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass: the one oracle file clears no bar; its consumer note says the failure needs the list when the rule says the fix does", "recommendation": "Set covered: .prawduct/changes/critic-good-faith-severity.md. I checked its claims against the diff and the base. Both Severity sections carry the cap (goals-1-3.md '## Severity', review-protocol.md '## Severity Levels'). The two lines are identical, and test_both_carriers_state_one_rule pins that. No consolidation code changed. The cuts are the ones the token-budget comments name. Two wording slips clear none of the three bars. (1) The Consumer note, which change_fragments.py ships into release notes, says reviews no longer rate 'any failure that needs ... a list to cover an open set'. The rule caps a finding 'whose fix needs a list to close an open set'. The garbling misleads no reader into an action. (2) The frontmatter is dated 2026-11-25, but the body cites the owner's ruling 'of 2026-11-26'. The ruling commit 5847f8b8 is 22:43 -0700 on the 25th, so this is a local-versus-UTC date and nothing reads it. No edit is wanted for either.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/critic-good-faith-severity.md"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "The cap's yield is not countable: a capped observation carries no title prefix", "recommendation": "The new test's docstring says this rule outranks every rule that raises a severity. The floor sentence is what stops it swallowing real failures. If reviewers over-apply it, a real defect gets demoted to an observation that looks like any other minor one. Nothing in the store can then separate the observations this cap produced from ordinary ones, so the owner cannot check whether the cap is suppressing real defects. The protocol makes its other judgment rules countable by a title prefix (`scope-trace:`, `read-cost:`, `rule-unenforced:`). A `good-faith:` prefix on observations rated down by this cap would do the same. This is offered as an option, not a defect.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md"], "oid": "O-9"}], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `fix/critic-good-faith-severity`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "chunks_declared": false, "chunks_evidence": {}, "base_reviewed": "05a1dceb997d0a861f951c64bc721e9939b3d6e8", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/changes/critic-good-faith-severity.md", "plugin/skills/critic/goals-1-3.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-protocol.md"], "chunk_graded": null, "chunks_graded": [], "plan_graded": null, "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": null, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0, "late-requirement-record": 0, "test-scope-conftest": 0, "test-scope-autouse": 0, "test-scope-init": 0, "test-clock-fake": 0, "test-clock-import": 0}}, "span": "full", "bounded_from": null, "span_reason": "no-boundary-review", "dispatched_at": "2026-11-26T06:05:33Z"}}

